== A Preface, or Brief Account of the Publishing of This Work

#align(center)[#emph[Written by its author, and dedicated to those whom God has counted him worthy to beget to faith by his ministry in the word]]

Children, grace be with you#super[+], #emph[Amen]. I am taken from you#super[+] in presence, and so tied up that I cannot perform the duty that lies on me from God toward you, for your further edifying and building up in faith and holiness, etc. Yet, so that you#super[+] may see that my soul has a fatherly care and desire for your spiritual and everlasting welfare, I now once again, as before from the top of Shenir and Hermon, so now from the lions’ dens and from the mountains of the leopards (S. of Song 4:8), still look after you#super[+] all, greatly longing to see your safe arrival into #emph[the] desired haven.

I thank God upon every remembrance of you#super[+], and rejoice, even while I stick between the teeth of the lions in the wilderness, at the grace, and mercy, and knowledge of Christ our Savior, which God has bestowed on you, with abundance of faith and love. Your#super[+] hungerings and thirstings also after further acquaintance with the Father, in His Son, your tenderness of heart, your trembling at sin, your sober and holy conduct also, before both God and men, are a great refreshment to me: “For you#super[+] are my glory and joy” (1 Thess 2:20).

I have sent you#super[+] here enclosed a drop of that honey that I have taken out of the carcass of a lion (Judg 14:5–9). I have eaten of it myself also, and am much refreshed by it. (Temptations, when we first meet them, are like the lion that roared at Samson; but if we overcome them, the next time we see them we shall find a nest of honey within them.) The Philistines do not understand me. It is something of a relation of the work of God upon my soul, from the very first till now, in which you#super[+] may perceive my castings down and risings up; for he wounds, and his hands make whole. It is written in the Scripture (Isa 38:19), “The father will make known the truth of God to the children.” Indeed, it was for this reason that I lay so long at Sinai (Deut 4:10, 11), to see the fire, and the cloud, and the darkness, that I might fear the Lord all the days of my life on earth, and tell of his wondrous works to my children (Ps 78:3–5).

Moses (Num 33:1, 2) wrote of the journeyings of the children of Israel from Egypt to the land of Canaan, and commanded also that they should remember their forty years’ travel in the wilderness: “Remember that these forty years the #smallcaps[Lord] your God led you all the way in the wilderness, so that He might humble you and test you in order to know what was in your heart, whether or not you would keep His commandments” (Deut 8:2). So this I have endeavored to do; and not only that, but to publish it also, so that, if God wills, others may be put in remembrance of what He has done for their souls, by reading His work upon me.

It is profitable for Christians to be often calling to mind the very beginnings of grace in their souls. “Because the #smallcaps[Lord] kept a vigil that night to bring them out of the land of Egypt, this same night is to be a vigil to the #smallcaps[Lord], to be observed by all the Israelites for the generations to come” (Exod 12:42). “My God,” says David (Ps 42:6), “my soul is cast down within me; but I will remember You from the land of Jordan and the peaks of Hermon, from the hill Mizar.” He remembered also the lion and the bear when he went to fight with the giant of Gath (1 Sam 17:36, 37).

It was Paul’s accustomed manner (Acts 22), and that when he was tried for his life (Acts 24), to open, even before his judges, the manner of his conversion: he would think of that day and that hour in which he first met with grace, for he found it supported him. When God had brought the children of Israel out of the Red Sea, far into the wilderness, yet they had to turn quite about to it again, to remember the drowning of their enemies there (Num 14:25). For though they sang His praise before, yet “they soon forgot His works” (Ps 106:11–13).

In this discourse of mine you#super[+] may see much; much, I say, of the grace of God toward me. I thank God I can count it much, for it was above my sins and Satan’s temptations too. I can remember my fears, and doubts, and sad months with comfort; they are as the head of Goliath in my hand. There was nothing to David like Goliath’s sword, that very sword that should have been sheathed in his own bowels; for the very sight and remembrance of it preached God’s deliverance to him. Oh, the remembrance of my great sins, of my great temptations, and of my great fears of perishing forever! They bring fresh into my mind the remembrance of my great help, my great support from heaven, and the great grace that God extended to such a wretch as I.

My dear children, call to mind the former days and the years of ancient times; remember also your#super[+] songs in the night; and commune with your own hearts (Ps 77:5–12). Indeed, look diligently, and leave no corner in them unsearched, for there is treasure hidden there, even the treasure of your#super[+] first and second experience of the grace of God toward you. Remember, I say, the word that first laid hold upon you#super[+]; remember your terrors of conscience, and fear of death and hell; remember also your tears and prayers to God; yes, how you sighed under every hedge for mercy. Have you#super[+] no hill Mizar to remember? Have you#super[+] forgotten the field, the milk house, the stable, the barn, and the like, where God visited your souls? Remember also the Word—the Word, I say, upon which the Lord has caused you#super[+] to hope. If you#super[+] have sinned against light; if you are tempted to blaspheme; if you are down in despair; if you think God fights against you; or if heaven is hidden from your eyes, remember it was so with your father, “but out of them all the Lord delivered me.”

I could have enlarged much in this discourse of mine on my temptations and troubles for sin, as also on the merciful kindness and working of God with my soul. I could also have stepped into a style much higher than this in which I have here written, and could have adorned all things more than I have here seemed to do, but I dare not. God did not play in convincing me, the devil did not play in tempting me, neither did I play when I sank as into a bottomless pit, when “the pangs of hell caught hold upon me”; so I may not play in relating them, but must be plain and simple, and lay down the thing as it was. He who likes it, let him receive it; and he who does not, let him produce a better. Farewell.

#emph[My dear children], the milk and honey is beyond this wilderness. God be merciful to you#super[+], and grant that you may not be slothful to go in to possess the land.
